           EFFECT OF NICKEL ADDITION ON THE AIR
         GASIFICATION OF OIL PALM MESOCARP FIBER
                                     1                       1               2
          SITI N. I. NOR-AZEMI , FARAH A. FUADI , S. ZHANG ,
                                                  1,
                     SYED SHATIR A. SYED-HASSAN *
            1
             Faculty of Chemical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi MARA,
                         40450 Shah Alam, Selangor, Malaysia
  2
   School of Chemical and Environmental Engineering, China Universiti of Mining and
           Technology (Beijing), D11 Xueyuen Road, Beijing 100083, China
                  *Corresponding Author: shatir@salam.uitm.edu.my




                                         Abstract
    Gasification is one of the efficient methods to convert low-value biomass into
    higher-value energy, fuel and chemical through the production of syngas. This
    study investigates the effect of direct nickel addition to biomass on the
    gasification of biomass. In this study, nickel was added to oil palm mesocarp
    fiber via ion-exchange with nickel nitrate solution. The Ni-loaded biomass was
    gasified inside a reactor at different temperatures (within 750-950oC) using air
    as the gasifying agent. To determine the effect of nickel addition on biomass
    gasification, comparison of syngas concentration was made between the
    gasification of Ni-loaded oil palm mesocarp fiber and that of the unloaded (raw)
    oil palm mesocarp fiber. Our results showed that Ni-loaded oil palm mesocarp
    fiber produces higher concentration of CH4, CO and H2 compared to the raw oil
    palm mesocarp fiber within the same range of reaction temperatures. The nickel
    addition onto biomass also reduces CO2 concentration in the product gas and
    lowers the amount of tar produced from the gasification of oil palm mesocarp
    fiber to only 0.35% of tar yield.
    Keywords: Air Gasification, Oil Palm Mesocarp Fiber, Nickel, Biomass.



1. Introduction
The high availability of biomass and its CO2-neutral characteristic open up a
possibility of developing future renewable and sustainable technologies for the
production of energy, fuel and chemical. The conversion of biomass can be done
through thermochemical and biological processes. The high efficiency of processes
either for small or large-scale system has gained interest of many researchers.

                                           79
80    S.N.I. Nor-Azemi et al.




  Abbreviations

  ER                   International Standard Atmosphere
  FTIR                 National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics
  GC                   World Health Organisation
  ICP-OES              Inductive Coupled Plasma Emission Spectroscopy
   Gasification is one of the thermochemical processing methods that can
convert biomass into valuable gaseous product such as CO and H2 that can be
used in Fischer-Tropsch synthesis for fuel and chemical production and also as
gaseous fuel for electricity generation [1].
    Gasification process usually needs high temperature (> 800 0C) for better
conversion and efficient reaction rates [2]. During gasification, the carbonaceous
materials are converted into syngas (mainly CO and H2) and aromatic
hydrocarbons as tar. The syngas needs costly cleaning operations before it can be
further utilized for various applications. Tar formation during gasification process
becomes the problem for further usage of the products gas and it can cause
blocking and fouling to the piping system, engines or turbines in downstream
processes. In order to enhance the tar removal and to increase the value of product
gases, catalyst is applied in the gasification process. Nickel is one of the transition
metals that is widely used as a catalyst due to its high efficiency, high activity and
high availability. Nickel catalysts were found to be effective in removing tar and
improving the syngas quality in steam/air gasification of biomass [3].
    Usually, catalyst is applied in the subsequent reactor (catalytic reactor) at the
downstream processing to remove tar. This is uneconomical since it utilizes
several reactors in the process. Gasification of metal-loaded biomass/coal was
studied in order to reduce the cost and results in high quality of the products.
Hurley et al. [2] reported that metal ions enhanced the combustible gas yield and
reduced the tar content during the air/CO2 gasification of pine sawdust. The
reduced metallic species of nickel added on lignocellulosic biomass was also
found to be highly favorable for H2 production [4]. In this study, the effect of
nickel addition onto oil palm mesocarp fiber (OPMF) on the gasification of
OPMF was investigated. Nickel was added directly onto the OPMF via ion-
exchange with nickel nitrate solution. The Ni-loaded OPMF was gasified at
different temperatures in a stainless steel updraft gasifier. The result showed that,
nickel addition gives positive results by increasing the concentration of product
gas and reducing the tar content compared with without the addition of nickel.


2. Materials and Methods
2.1. Biomass preparation and characterization
Samples of OPMF were collected from Sime Darby R&D Center, Carey Island,
Malaysia. The OPMF were sun dried to remove undesired moisture content for 2
days before being further dried in an oven for 24 h at 110 oC. The dried OPMF
were then ground using a high speed rotary grinder and sieved properly to provide
constant particles size (125-300μm). The samples were placed in closed
containers for further use. The functional group of Ni-loaded and unloaded OPMF
were analysed using Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectrometer (Perkin-


Journal of Engineering Science and Technology                 Special Issue 7 1/2015
 Effect of Nickel Addition on the Air Gasification of Oil Palm Mesocarp Fiber     81



Elmer 2000). In order to determine the phase of nickel species attached on the
OPMF, X-ray Diffraction (XRD) were carried out using Rigaku Ultima IV(Cu Kα
radiation, 40 kV/40 mA). The crystallite size of nickel species was determined
using Scherrer equation.


2.2. Nickel loading experiment
10 g of Nickel nitrate hexahydrate, Ni(NO3)2.6H2O, (Merck) was used to prepare
nickel stock solution by dissolving it in 1200 mL distilled water. 200g of raw
Ni(NO3)2.6H2O OPMF was added into the mixture and stirred by a magnetic
stirrer in a beaker for 120 min. The mixture was filtered and the solid sample was
rinsed with distilled water several times until the filtrate has a constant pH and no
nickel detected using ICP-OES (iCAP 6000 Series). The solid samples were then
digested by using acid digestion method in a microwave digester (Milestone
ETHOS). The digested solution was analyzed using inductive coupled plasma
optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) (iCAP 6000 Series).


2.3. Air gasification experiment
In order to study the effect of nickel addition on the produced gas, the Ni-loaded
and Niunloaded OPMF were gasified inside a stainless steel reactor. The biomass
was continuously fed into the reactor using a screw feeder driven by a variable
electric motor. Figure 1 shows the setup for air gasification experiments. The
gasifier, with an inner diameter of 2.2 cm, an outer diameter of 2.7 cm and a total
height of 45 cm was heated inside a tube furnace. A thermocouple was placed
inside the reactor to determine the reactor temperature. The experiment was
repeated using different temperature (750ºC, 800ºC, 850ºC, 900ºC, and 950ºC) for
both Ni-loaded and unloaded samples.
    Air was introduced in the gasification process as a gasifying agent with the
equivalent ratio (ER) of 0.2 and the flow rate of 1.2 L/min (controlled by a flow
meter). The feed flow rate of the OPMF sample was calibrated 5 times in order
to establish consistent rate of 2.3 g/min. The mixture of methanol and
chloroform with the ratio of 1:4 was placed inside three dreschel bottles to act
as a gas cooler and a tar trapper. The produced gas was collected from the outlet
of the last dreschel bottle using gas sampling bags. For each test, the gas
samples were analyzed offline by a gas chromatography (GC)
(ThermoFinnigan) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) using
argon as a carrier gas. The measurement was repeated three times using GC and
the average values were taken to represent the gas components for a particular
gas sample.
    For tar analysis, approximately 4 g of solution out of 120 g (from the first
dreschel bottle) was put into a beaker and being dried in an oven at 40 oC for 4 h.
The solvent was evaporated completely at this temperature, leaving the heavier
organic compounds (tar) remain in the beaker. The blank sample (mixture of
methanol and chloroform) was also prepared with the same amount and steps as a
reference. The tar amount and the tar yield were calculated from the initial and the
final weight of the beaker by Eqs. (1) and (2), respectively. Y in the equations
represents the total tar per gram of solution.




